According to Mr. Phay Siphan, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and a spokesman for the Royal Government, the Pursat 1 Stung Hydropower Project is a priority project that the Royal Government has included in the master plan for the development of the electricity sector to be launched this year. 2023 and started the study process since 2010.
The amount of work that the company has to build includes: dams, pipelines, roads, 80 MW power station, 230 kV high voltage transmission line, 5 km long from Stung Pursat 1 hydropower station to connect with 230 kV high voltage transmission line. Existing from Osom Substation to Pursat Substation and other secondary infrastructure.
The construction of Stung Pursat 1 hydropower plant is done according to the construction-operation-transfer (BOT) model, with a concession period of 39 years, of which 4 years for construction and 35 years for operation. At the end of 35 years of operation, the company will return the hydropower to the Royal Government.
The company has prepared a budget of nearly $ 8 million to address these issues. Of which, the total budget of 1,685,980 USD for environmental rehabilitation is: 706,000 USD for water and land protection, 75,000 USD for environmental protection, 826,480 USD for environmental and social fund, $ 53,500 for health and $ 25,000 for environmental training and outreach.
Separately, $ 6.83 million will be used to address the policy by providing compensation to people affected by the investment project.
However, the Stung Pursat 1 hydropower investment project is expected to bring many benefits to the people and economy of Cambodia. According to Phay Siphan, the project will generate income for local people at the provincial and national levels by providing a lot of jobs during construction and operation, some people can also generate income from their own professional occupations. Related to project activities.
The government will receive an average of $ 5 million in annual tax revenue. This does not include other taxes that EDC and other electricity distributors have to pay to the state for distribution to consumers.
For the solar power plant project in Krakor district, Pursat province and in Teuk Phos district, Kampong Chhnang province, which provides a total power of 120 MW, there is no impact on the environment and people's livelihood. On the contrary, this project contributes to many benefits for the people and the national economy.
