He added that the increase of vehicles, the growth of people, as well as the development of traffic congestion on a regular basis, the Provincial Department of Public Works has regularly joined forces to facilitate the challenges that may cause accidents on the road, improve traffic lights and distribution. Traffic education law books, as well as inviting car companies to educate about road traffic laws.
The report added that, in fact, the strategic action plan for the road safety sector in Kampong Speu province, which is in line with the direction of the national road safety policy, which reflects the efforts and considerations of the main instability, the lives of the people and reduce the risk. Travel the streets at a low level.
The plan also contributes to reducing poverty and raising awareness of the rule of law through education, guidance, and implementation, which is a factor in supporting national economic development and in line with the time of integration in the ASEAN community. This is the additional confirmation of Mr. Hong Chan Sokha.
It should be noted that on the morning of April 4, 2019, yesterday, there was a horrific traffic accident on National Road No. 44 in Kampong Speu Province between a truck and a truck carrying workers, causing 12 workers to suffer serious and minor injuries. Eight people were seriously injured. What is more serious and tragic is that five workers lost their arms.
According to the General Commissioner of Kampong Speu, the accident was caused by a large truck driving a truck carrying workers. After the accident, the driver left the car and fled, while the injured worker was taken to hospital.
At the same time, please call on workers who ride in the car must be very careful not to put their hands out of the car to avoid traffic accidents such as the above case, which caused the loss of their hands due to this negligence.
According to a senior official of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, since the case crossed into Ratanakkiri province on March 22, 2019, to date, about 400 pigs have been killed.
Both Ratanakkiri provincial officials and the Ministry of Agriculture have confirmed that the African swine fever epidemic in Ratanakkiri is not due to the import of pigs from Vietnam, as the province does not have a permit to import pigs, but may be due to ethnic groups. Vietnam transported all kinds of vegetables and meat to sell in this province to infect pigs in Cambodia.
Mr. Soy Sona, Director of the Ratanakkiri Provincial Department of Agriculture, confirmed that in Ratanakkiri, there are no pigs imported from Vietnam, and the infected pigs are from the pigs of the people released in the village. Officials are investigating immediately.
African swine fever, which the Vietnamese and Lao governments have been cracking down on, was discovered by Cambodian officials in Som Kaninh village, Som Thom commune, O'Yadao district, Ratanakkiri province, but the Ministry of Agriculture and authorities Immediate measures are being taken to prevent the spread of this disease.
However, a senior official at the Ministry of Health and spokesman Ly Sovann claimed that the disease was not contagious and could not be transmitted to humans, so called on all residents not to worry.
On April 2, 2019, Minister of Agriculture Veng Sakhon also issued a proclamation identifying the African swine fever epidemic zone in Som Kaninh village. According to the minister, the disease is not contagious and does not affect humans, but causes pigs to get sick and die at a high rate of up to 100 percent.
At the same time, the Minister also confirmed that the raising, purchase, sale and transportation of pigs, pork and products of all kinds of pigs in the affected areas have been temporarily suspended.
In addition to the announcement of the Minister of Agriculture on the morning of April 5, 2019, Mr. Tan Phannara, Royal Government delegate and Director General of the General Department of Animal Health and Animal Production, confirmed that the Ministry has collected pigs in the future. At the site of the outbreak, about 100 animals were killed and burned, and measures were taken to prevent pig traffic within 3 kilometers and further research within 10 kilometers.
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of April 5, 2019, Ms. Phat Pov, the biological mother of Miss Dum Rida, entered the Phnom Penh Municipal Court on the summons of Investigating Judge Mr. Bunthy in connection with the case of negligent driving. Carelessness causes the death of others under Article 85 of the Road Traffic Law.
The appearance of the mother of Miss Dom Rida to the Phnom Penh Municipal Court with the purpose of confirming before the judge the acceptance of $ 70,000 in civil damages from the family of the perpetrator, Mr. Yin Ngich, the father of Ms. Yin Mana, and Withdrawal of claim (civil case). Please confirm that Ms. Yin Mana is still serving her sentence.
Please be informed that on the evening of April 3, 2019, the family of Miss Dum Rida agreed to accept $ 70,000 in civil damages from the family of the perpetrator, Mr. Yin Ngich, who drove the car and hit Ms. Dum Rida, causing her immediate death. At the scene in Toul Kork, Phnom Penh a few days ago.
This agreement was made without any coercion in the office of Samdech Techo Sen at the Office of the Council of Ministers and in the presence of lawyers on both sides as witnesses.
The victim's mother filed a complaint with the support of Samdech Techo Hun Sen's volunteer lawyers. A day after the tragic accident, lawyers went to the ceremony and helped protect the family of the victim who was preparing for the funeral of her daughter at home.
On the afternoon of March 29, 2019, Mr. Bunthy, Investigating Judge of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court, issued an order to bring Ms. Yin Mana, who drove a luxury car and hit the best student, to the Phnom Penh PJ Prison to await the trial. Clear the sentence later according to the law.
The victim, 22-year-old Dum Rida, was born in Kampong Damrey village, Prek Tamak commune, Khsach Kandal district, Kandal province. The perpetrator was named Yin Mana, the daughter of Mr. Yin Ngich.
