At the same time, the Director of the Phnom Penh Municipal Health Department, Dr. Ngy Mean Heng, confirmed many achievements in 2018 and is committed to setting the direction for 2019 to be even better in improving the health of the general population, including maternal health. Infants, children and nutrition on women and children and many other public health issues.
Deputy Prime Minister Men Sam An sets out eight priority tasks for the Ministry of National Assembly-Senate Relations and Inspection to implement more effectively
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of National Assembly-Senate Relations and Inspection on April 4, 2019 successfully concluded its 2018 Annual Meeting and successfully proposed its work direction for 2019 under the chairmanship of Ms. Kittisangkhab Dr. Men Sam An, Deputy Director General. Minister at Sokha Phnom Penh Hotel, Khan Chroy Changva, Phnom Penh.
On that occasion, Ms. Men Sam An requested the leadership of the Ministry to continue to focus on the following eight important tasks:
1. The working group of the Ministry must continue the campaign to educate and disseminate the law widely in all 25 capitals and provinces, especially in remote urban and rural areas. Encourage survey work by training survey study methods and data analysis to relevant officials to report on the findings of those surveys.
2. To continue to coordinate with the relevant ministries and institutions to finalize the main parts of the draft law, inspection and the relevant sub-decree. That facilitation work must fully protect the core roles and responsibilities of the Ministry in accordance with the law.
3. To set up and install an online complaint application program and e-Library, through which people can easily file complaints and view legal documents and legal documents via computer or mobile phone. The implementation of the program is to facilitate the provision of public services to the people to strengthen good governance and contribute to the fight against corruption and protect the rights of citizens.
5: Civil servants of the Ministry, especially officials or inspection agencies, must find out about the laws and regulations related to the core work of the Ministry in order to be able to interpret and explain the findings of inactivity.
6. The National Inspection Institute shall establish a board and a committee to organize the content of the training program for primary, secondary and higher education on inspection and related skills. The institute also provides training in other fields besides inspection, such as education, dissemination, law, research, evaluation, surveying and other skills that are required by the Ministry.
7. Prepare to implement the Information Technology for Financial Management (FMIS) system to strengthen public financial management must be thorough, especially the use of budgets to be effective, accountable and in accordance with legal standards.
