The first Chroy Changvar Bridge is an important bridge connecting Phnom Penh across the Tonle Sap River, which was built and commissioned in 1963 through a grant from the Government of Japan. The bridge is 971 meters long and 13 meters wide.
The first Chroy Changvar Bridge, which served as the main crossing for the Tonle Sap, was cut off during 1972, when Cambodia was engulfed in war. During that time, it was very difficult for people to travel by boat or ferry across this huge river.
Until 1992, when Cambodia was in the process of seeking peace and unity between Cambodians and Cambodians from all parties to the conflict, the first Chroy Changvar Bridge was repaired and reconnected by Japan and renamed Cambodia-Japan Friendship Bridge by His Majesty the King, His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk.
By the end of 2017, due to the fact that the bridge is getting older and the economic growth and traffic flow is increasing, the bridge needs to be repaired urgently. The bridge is scheduled to be completed by the end of 2019.
Due to the efforts of the repair work of both Japan and Cambodia, working day and night, also made the repair ahead of schedule and chose to re-launch on April 3 to facilitate people's travel during National New Year.
The repair of the bridge cost about 30 million US dollars, a grant from the Government of Japan. Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, emphasized that the repair of the bridge was of good quality under the high attention of Japanese and Cambodian repair engineers.
Japanese officials claim that after the renovation, the Cambodia-Japan Friendship Bridge can be used for another 50 years.
In order to increase the awareness of the use of the road to the people of Cambodia, on the newly repaired Chroy Changvar Bridge, there are also drawings of motorbikes for motorbike roads and people walking on the sidewalks. In addition, students are invited to visit the bridge and educate them on the use of the road on the bridge.
Please note that in order to facilitate the celebration of the reopening of the Cambodia-Japan Friendship Bridge today, the Phnom Penh Capital Administration has also decided to avoid traffic during the ribbon cutting ceremony.
