(Phnom Penh): The Asian Development Bank (ADB) on April 3, 2019, released a new report predicting Cambodia's economic growth, claiming that the Cambodian economy remains strong but needs to close the skills gap. .
Due to the economic growth of developed countries and the People's Republic of China, Cambodia's main export market is projected to slow down, Cambodia's export growth and tourism are likely to slow slightly compared to last year. This is according to the Asia Development Outlook (ADO) 2019, ADB's special annual economic report.
The report suggests that with economic growth expected to slow slightly, Cambodia's current account deficit should narrow this year and next. Inflation is expected to average 2.5% for 2019 and 2020. High inflows of investment will help offset current account deficits and accumulate a total of about $ 12 billion in international reserves in 2019 to cover imports for about six months.
The 2019 ADO report identifies a major challenge to the skills gap that arises from the mismatch between the skills that the workforce has and the skills that employers need. Cambodia needs more high quality teachers and needs to provide financial support to poor students who excel to help them continue their education and learn the skills needed in the digital age.
