Opinion: Two bridges spanning the Tonle Sap River show the strength of Samdech Techo Sen's "international diplomatic policy"
(Phnom Penh): Anti-strong men Techo Sen has devised all means to attack the man who has been the Prime Minister of Cambodia for more than 30 years. While the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia was building a close friendship with China, the world's second largest economy, Hun Sen's opponents attacked and accused him of being a biased communist. China goes to dictatorship.
Such accusations are not limited to the present, in the 1980s the Cambodian strongman was also charged. Samdech Techo Hun Sen once reminded that in the 1980s, when Samdech visited Mr. Serey's country, he was accused of being a "communist leader" and when he visited a communist country, he was accused of being a "free leader".
Samdech Techo Hun Sen emphasized that it was a painful feeling for Samdech. But what keeps Samdech strong on the battlefield of his leadership is the realism and self-awareness that Samdech is the leader of Cambodia and does everything for the Cambodian people, regardless of who accuses Samdech of being human. Free or communist.
Is Samdech Techo Sen now a biased, diplomatic leader and a loner as alleged?
Cambodia was known to have been politically and economically besieged by liberal countries, including China, during the 1980s, but at that time Cambodia was very close to the Soviet Union, the world's largest brother. And there are sharp conflicts with other great powers, such as the United States and the liberal country.
But after the Paris Agreement of October 23, 1991, Cambodia also actively and actively integrated into the region and the world under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, a former diplomat.
Cambodia joined ASEAN on April 30, 1999, became a member of the World Trade Organization in September 2009, and formed an alliance with South Korea on October 30, 1997. As Cambodia became closer to North Korea, South Korea's conflicting parties all showed intelligence in the diplomacy of strong man Techo Sen.
Road and bridge infrastructure is a priority under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, which not only facilitates the movement of people, but also helps boost the Cambodian economy. The construction of Cambodia's national roads and bridges is not yet affordable, leading to the search for funds, both aid and loans from friendly countries.
China and Japan, historical rivals and economic rivals and the most influential in Asia today, are the largest lenders and grants for road and bridge infrastructure in Cambodia.
Currently, we can see that National Road No. 5 connects Phnom Penh to Poipet, the Cambodian-Thai border, which is under construction and expanding under a Japanese loan. National Road No. 6 connecting Phnom Penh to Siem Reap was built with a loan from the Chinese government. In addition, the two national roads that cross the Tonle Sap and the Tonle Sap Lake are connected by two bridges.
A bridge was built during the Sangkum Reast era in the 1960s and must be maintained and repaired under the current regime of Samdech Techo Hun Sen. Another bridge was built with a Chinese government loan and opened in October 2015. Both bridges are playing an important role in transporting Cambodians across the great river in Phnom Penh.
The presence of these road and bridge infrastructure systems will not be able to be present on Cambodian soil, even in terms of aid or loans, if the Cambodian leader, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, does not have good diplomatic relations and lacks intelligence. In building relations with Japan and China.
The presence of roads as well as two bridges lying parallel to the Tonle Sap River shows that Cambodia's strongman, Techo Sen, is not a leader who has a political policy, diplomacy, loneliness or bias towards any country. In particular, showed the strength of the diplomatic battle of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, which can attract the sympathy of the conflicting countries and rivals of Sino-Japanese influence to build roads and bridges to serve the interests of Cambodia.
