Minister Vong Soth: Celebrating the Day of Autism and Down Syndrome is to Encourage the Mentally Impaired to Communicate and Promote Participation in Social Activities
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia, as well as the world, has set April 2 every year to celebrate the Day of Autism and Down Syndrome, also known as dementia, to encourage all intellectuals to Communicate with the general public as well as promote participation in social activities.
According to research, the disease is more common in children under 3 years of age and is higher in boys than in girls. This was stated by Mr. Vong Soth, Minister of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation on the Day of Autism and Down Syndrome, also known as the International Dementia 2019, under the theme "Eliminating barriers to the participation of people with autism and Down Syndrome." "
This important day was organized by the General Secretariat of the Disability Action Council in collaboration with the Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation and national and international non-governmental organizations involved in the field of disability at the Hun Sen Regional Teacher Training Center in Kandal Province on April 2. 2019.
The Minister of Social Affairs said that in order to show the will and attention of the Royal Government of Cambodia and development partners to promote support and encourage people with disabilities to give them the opportunity to fully enjoy and participate. In social development activities.
At the same time, Minister Vong Soth added that the Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation, the Council for the Action of Persons with Disabilities has actively worked to address the challenges facing people with autism and Down Syndrome and to promote a society without barriers. Environment into economic, social, artistic, cultural, and political activities with dignity and non-discrimination.
Mr. Em Chanmakara, Secretary General of the Council for the Action of Persons with Disabilities, said that the participation of people with intellectual disabilities and autism is very important to achieve the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), which the Royal Government of Cambodia is strongly committed to. In achieving this development goal.
In Cambodia, this day is celebrated for the sixth time in order to encourage people with dementia and autism to become independent, self-determined and support the support of the community and family, especially teachers with intellectual disabilities. Because this type of disability can not be treated in the short term, it is necessary to use long-term care and treatment.
