(Phnom Penh): Youk Chhang, director of the Documentation Center of Cambodia, said that those who tried to forget the Khmer Rouge genocidal regime were like those who wanted such a regime to happen again. He was speaking to Fresh News during a training session for university professors on "Genocide and Massacre in Global History" on April 2, 2019.
Mr. Youk Chhang added that due to forgetting and not studying the history, especially this brutal genocidal regime of the world, Cambodia has experienced this dark regime. Experience has shown that genocide has increased long before it occurred in Cambodia. This regime first appeared in Armenia in the 20th century, in 1915, and is now 104 years old.
After Armenia, Germany, Bosnia, Rwanda and finally Cambodia, the genocidal regime in Cambodia was the most brutal regime because it not only killed other nations but also its own nation.
For this reason, Mr. Youk Chhang, Director of the Documentation Center of Cambodia, as well as the Ministry of Education, have collaborated to launch the training of higher level teachers to better understand the genocidal regime in order to make it easier for students to teach in addition to the brutal regime. This.
Unlike Youk Chhang, who is 66 years old, Morn Mao, who lives in Oddar Meanchey province, is one of the plaintiffs who filed a lawsuit against the Khmer Rouge tribunal. He said that during the genocide, he lost his uncle Killed by the Khmer Rouge, not only did he have no hope of survival.
Through personal experience and suffering, Mon Mao often told his descendants about his starving life awaiting his death under the Khmer Rouge regime. He did this so that the next generation would be able to persevere and not want this regime to return in Cambodia as well as the whole world.
At the same time, one of the many victims who lost their relatives in the genocidal regime wants more teaching time for the younger generation, as well as keeping them in other areas where prisons are a brutal killing field. Of the Khmer Rouge. In order for the next generation to know and understand this brutal genocidal regime.
The Khmer Rouge genocidal regime, also known as the Pol Pot era, began in Cambodia on April 17, 1975, and collapsed on January 7, 1979, during the liberation of the United National United Front for National Salvation. Co-led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister.
