Rural Development Bank claims to provide credit to silk producers in Phnom Srok district if they regroup to resume this work
(Banteay Meanchey): The Director General of the Rural Development Bank, Mr. Kao Thach, has promised to provide loans at the most favorable interest rates to silk artisans in Phnom Srok district, Banteay Meanchey province, if they together form a Phnom Srok silk production community to continue the legacy work. From this ancestor again.
Phnom Srok district, Banteay Meanchey province, is a place that left a legacy of silk production from ancestors, but after a while, very few families are still engaged in this silk production business.
Therefore, on the afternoon of March 31, 2019, the Director General of the Rural Development Bank attended a meeting with silk artisans to discuss the reasons for the artisans to give up this business and find a solution to this problem.
Mr. Kao Thach encouraged artisans to reorganize into silk production communities, with the Rural Development Bank providing guaranteed credit assistance with the most favorable interest rates to benefit and create jobs for local artisans and avoid migration. .
He helps to find technicians to help teach or send volunteer farmers to study with technicians to teach to other artisans.
After the discussion, the silk artisans thanked Mr. Kao Thach for his attention, especially for Mr. Kao Thach's request, the artisans will consider it and will respond soon.
On the morning of March 31, 2019, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, inspected the quality of the bridge for the next time. After a thorough inspection in the heat of the day, the senior minister confirmed that the quality of the repairs was 100 percent good, of which the recipe paint was also imported directly from Japan.
Minister Sun Chanthol thanked the Japan International Cooperation Agency (JICA) for its efforts to repair the bridge day and night ahead of schedule.
With the inspection of the quality of the bridge, Mr. Sun Chanthol appealed to the transporters to help maintain the bridge without overloading and also called on the people to respect the traffic rules and drive carefully. Danger to life while driving to visit the hometown during the upcoming New Year.
It should be noted that the Cambodia-Japan Friendship Bridge, 971 meters long and 13 meters wide, located along National Road 6A in Phnom Penh, was built and inaugurated in 1963 under a Japanese grant. The bridge was damaged by the Cambodian Civil War and was rebuilt in 1992 by Japan and renamed the Cambodia-Japan Friendship Bridge by His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk.
Due to the age of the bridge, and as economic growth and traffic flow have steadily increased, the bridge needs to be repaired urgently at a cost of about 30 million US dollars under a grant from the Japanese government. The renovation process started on September 15, 2017.
The repair is scheduled to be completed in July 2019, but due to the efforts and attention day and night, the repair of the bridge was completed ahead of schedule. The bridge features road markings for cars, motorbikes and pedestrians as additional markings for road users.
In addition, both the Ministry of Public Works and Transport as well as JICA took students on a tour of the bridge and explained to them how to use the road.
Khmer Civilization Expert: What is the history of Chheng Meng Festival?
The tombs are like roof tiles, and the colored paper that covers the tombs represents business and coolness. This means that the festival is not only a reminder of ancestors, but also a recognition of oneself as a descendant of Chinese ancestors.
The history of the Cheng Meng Festival is related to the weather between the beginning of spring, the spring and the end of winter, from the Zhou Dynasty about 2,500 years ago. Because this festival is the season when farmers have already harvested their fields, the weather every four months on the 4-5th of each year is geographically only 15 degrees. This means that the festival is related to the weather and the harvest.
Therefore, according to Chinese tradition, during the Chheng Meng festival, when offering graves, the children have to prepare food, fruits, wine, and paper offerings to the grave. At the beginning, burn a piece of parchment paper, cut a few young green twigs, put them on the grave, worship, and then have a happy meal before returning home.
