Visiting his hometown, borrowing a gun, shooting a friend, was arrested by the authorities
(Tbong Khmum): A young blacksmith borrowed a friend's pistol to visit his hometown and fired a shot, causing a surprise and was arrested by the authorities. This incident happened at 8 pm on March 27, 2019 in Slap Ktong village, Chob commune, Tbong Khmum district.
The suspect was identified as Heng Sophea, alias Ouk, 24, a blacksmith at Chamkar Dong market, Sangkat Dangkor, Khan Dangkor, Phnom Penh.
Sources from the scene said that before the incident, they did not know what the problem was, but the suspect, Heng Sophea alias Ouk, who was not a welder, did not know where the pistol came from. The bullets caused an uproar throughout the night.
The source said that the next day, at 5:30 pm on March 28, 2019, the specialized force of Tbong Khmum District Police Inspectorate, in cooperation with the administrative police force, arrested the suspect and brought him for questioning by confiscating small arms. 1 original, brand number S2022, made in Turkey, paid for 3 tablets.
In front of the police, the suspect confessed that he borrowed the weapon from a friend who was a blacksmith and had only known each other for 4 months to visit the district. At 8 pm on March 27, 2019, he fired a shot at his father's house, then the police came down to arrest him.
Currently, the above suspects have been authorized to build a case and send it to the provincial court to proceed with the procedure. Legal Awareness: Staff Delegates
In all enterprises and establishments that fall under the provisions of the Labor Law and employ eight or more workers, a delegation of staff shall be established. Establishing a staff representative in an enterprise is to represent a single worker.
What is a staff representative? And what are the roles of employees in the enterprise and the establishment?
Employee delegates refer to workers' representatives through direct election of workers. (Article 4 of the Law on Trade Unions)
Pursuant to Article 1 of the Prakas of the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, the types of enterprises and establishments that need to have a staff representative are: industrial, commercial, mining, construction, banking services, agriculture, ports, hotels, restaurants, schools, schools and other production.
The number of staff delegates shall be determined in proportion to the number of workers in the following enterprises:
The election of staff representatives is the responsibility of the business owner, with the employer having to invite workers' representatives to discuss the procedure for assigning the date of the election and the number of seats to be allocated to the electorate. When an enterprise has one or more unions, they can nominate candidates based on the number of seats of the full-time and part-time staff delegates.
In the case of workers who are not members of the union, they can also stand as candidates, full-time staff representatives and assistant staff representatives. Employers must discuss with those unions and workers' representatives to determine the procedure, date and number of seats to be allocated to the electorate.
In particular, in the case of enterprises and establishments that do not have unions or do not have volunteer workers to stand or can not meet the conditions to stand as candidates, full staff representatives or assistant staff representatives, the Minister of Labor and Vocational Training must address this issue.
The following procedures for the election of staff delegates:
Article 2 of the Prakas of the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training
Roles and responsibilities of the staff delegates include:
Full Delegates and Assistant Delegates are elected for a two (2) year term and may be re-elected. The duties of an employee delegate are terminated upon death, resignation, and termination of the employment contract. (Article 41 of the Law on Trade Unions)
The dismissal of an employee delegate or a candidate to be an employee delegate must be approved by the Labor Inspector. Dismissal of staff may be made through:
1. Dismissal of Employee Delegation by Employer:
The dismissal of an employee representative or a candidate who has been nominated for an employee delegation must be approved by the Labor Inspectorate. This rule applies to former staff delegates after the end of their term of 3 months and within 3 months to former candidates who are not elected after the announcement of election results.
In the event of a serious mistake, the director of the enterprise may issue a decision immediately, but it is only a temporary rule to wait for the decision of the Labor Inspector. If the decision of the Labor Inspector decides not to terminate the employment of the applicant, the position is annulled and the decision of the owner of the enterprise is legally revoked.
2. Dismissal of staff delegates by workers:
Workers may fire their delegates before the end of the term in case the delegates representing the workers do not perform their duties. The decision on the eviction shall be made in the same manner as in the election procedure.
Workers must formally notify the Labor Inspector within three days after the decision to fire the employee delegate. Once a full-time delegate has been elected, all the duties of a full-time delegate are replaced by a full-time delegate until a new mandate is elected.
Please also note that the delegates who were fired by the workers are still workers in the same enterprise.
In short, staff delegates are workers' representatives elected directly by workers and are formed through elections. Eligible candidates must be at least 25 years old and have been employed for at least six (6) months in the enterprise in which they work.
The elected staff delegates have a two-year term and can run for re-election. The duties of a staff delegate are terminated by death at the time of resignation and termination of contract.
