LOS ANGELES (Reuters) - A helicopter that NASA plans to take to Mars has finally successfully completed a test run of an automated system and a virtual flight under conditions similar to that of Mars. . This was released by the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Saturday evening, March 30, 2019.
Preparations for its test run began in late January 2019, eight months after the announcement of plans to send the mini-helicopter to Mars, which will be launched from Earth during 2020 or 2021. Face as it is set not to weigh more than 1.8 kg.
It will be a small aircraft that can float itself in the air of Mars, and it will also be a robot to test which aircraft are capable of flying in a thin Mars atmosphere. More than 100 times more than Earth's atmosphere.
In addition, Mars has a very weak gravity and a very thin atmosphere, as well as a strong dust explosion, so it is very complicated to produce a helicopter that is small but can move in a thin atmosphere. Which can withstand such dust storms. As we all know, even when the weather is bad on Earth, some helicopters on Earth can not even resist, how about a dust storm on Mars and no man-made command?
