Tony Kranh, Acting Director of the Office of Administration of the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia, said this was another tribunal achievement for the Khmer Rouge tribunal in seeking justice and truth for the victims of the Khmer Rouge regime.
"The Royal Government of Cambodia, under the wise leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, has established the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia, inviting the United Nations to join the trial of former senior leaders and those responsible for crimes," he said. "The most serious of the Democratic Kampuchea regime for serious violations of national and international law, which took place between April 17, 1975 and January 6, 1979."
Mr. Kranh Tony added that the new achievements add to the many achievements that the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia have achieved over the past 12 years, both within and outside the jurisdiction. Legacy is considered to be the most important factor that should be preserved and passed on to the next generation, including education, memory, as well as capacity building and strengthening of the judiciary and the judiciary.
Mr. Kranh Tony said that these achievements can happen with the support of the Royal Government led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen. Honorary Legal Officer Dr. Bin Chin continued his work in this work, and we always remember the contribution and important role of Samdech Vibol Panha Sok An in drafting laws as well as negotiating agreements to establish the UN. The ECCC is a court with a historic mandate.
The purpose of this trial is to seek justice, truth and remembrance within the framework of peace, political stability and national unity, which is an important way to respect and remember all victims to help strengthen the rule of law in Cambodia. In Cambodia and to ensure that such tragedies do not happen again on Cambodian soil or anywhere in the world.
Mr. Kranh Tony stated that until the Khmer Rouge tribunal achieved many results in bringing justice to the victims who died during the Khmer Rouge regime and the survivors. Finding the truth for all of humanity, strengthening the rule of law, eliminating impunity, national reconciliation, reconciling the victims and building the capacity of officials and staff, as well as providing more legacy for the justice system in Cambodia.
It should be noted that on 16 November 2018, the Trial Chamber made a public statement of its findings and decisions in Case 002/02 at the ECCC Trial Chamber. . And sentenced both defendants to life in prison.
