(Siem Reap): Groundwater and surface conditions are very important for temples in the Angkor area, especially Angkor Wat. Therefore, maintaining a balance of water in both the dry and wet seasons in Kasinthu is essential, requiring proper water management to sustain the temple and maintain the beauty of the entire resort.
In fact, in the dry season in 2019, Cambodia will be affected by El Nino, causing hot and dry weather, causing a lot of water loss due to evaporation to the atmosphere, which has greatly affected the water management in the region. Angkor Resort.
Mr. Phoeun Sokhim, Deputy Director of Water Management Department of APSARA Authority, said, “Every day, about 10,000 cubic meters of water in Angkor Wat is lost (about 1/2 shares) by evaporation to the atmosphere and Water seepage into the soil. Although the evaporation is large, it does not affect the amount of water in the Angkor Wat temple basin, because the basin can hold a total of more than 1 million cubic meters of water, as well as regular water refills.
On the other hand, APSARA Authority also cooperates with Siem Reap Water Supply Authority on a regular basis to manage groundwater so that groundwater pumping does not affect the groundwater layer, which may pose a risk to conservation. Temples in the Angkor resort area.
In addition, the APSARA Authority has restored the ancient waterway system for drainage from Angkor Thom, Angkor Thom to Angkor Wat, at the point of the Om River east through the Sampov Loun canal. Therefore, the water in the temple of Angkor Wat ensures balance, does not dry out and maintains the beauty in the temple.
