Farmers in Kandeang commune, Kandeang district can produce rice three times a year due to good irrigation system
(Pursat): Kandeang Irrigation System is located in Sathan Village, Kandeang Commune, Kandeang District, about 10 km north of Pursat City. The irrigation system was built in 1985 and was damaged by annual floods, but was repaired by the Japanese government under an ODA project.
A farmer named Pil He claims that farmers living in the Kandeang district irrigation project site like him can produce rice three times a year because the Kandeang irrigation system can supply enough water in the dry months. He said that the rice that farmers like to grow is called Sen Kraoom, which currently costs 1,170 riel.
He added that in the dry season, farmers can produce rice twice, the first time from November to February and the second time from March to July. As for the rainy season, he confirmed that it is simple because it is the rainy season, which is favorable for rice production.
Meanwhile, the director of the Pursat Provincial Department of Water Resources and Meteorology, Mr. Keo Vay, said that there are 690 families living in the Kandieng district irrigation project, which is about 3,200 people. He said the irrigation system has a total length of 2,130 meters and has been restored with the assistance of the Japanese government of more than 80,000 US dollars.
According to the director of the Pursat Provincial Department of Water Resources, he added that through the ODA project, the function of agricultural water supply has been strengthened to irrigate the area and expanded from 280 hectares to 400 hectares. Therefore, the amount harvested per hectare during the rainy season increased from 1 ton to 3.5 tons per hectare.
As for the farmer, Pil He said that in 2019, he could not produce rice twice in the rainy season because the Royal Government announced not to produce because of fear of water use problems. The farmer said that the fragrant rice that farmers produce until the price of 1,000 riel or more to make a profit, but if the price falls below 1,000 riel is a loss.
He added, however, that people were more prosperous after the Japanese government restored the irrigation system. He said that in addition to water to make rice, farmers can also use the water to grow vegetables and other crops.
