(Mondulkiri): Often in the summer, the presence of a herd of wild elephants appears near the village of residents in Keo Seima district, Mondulkiri province. Residents said that on March 25, 2019, a herd of four wild elephants entered the area of Keo Seima District Hall and they never persecuted the people, they just came back. Its routine.
Keo Seima district officials filmed the herd in action as they entered the Keo Seima district compound. In a nearly two-minute video of the activities of four elephants that day, they apparently wanted to dive and drink in a pond in Keo Seima district hall, but did not go down into the pond with no way down and they returned. Go back into the deep forest without being chased by the people.
Keo Sopheak, director of the Mondulkiri provincial environment department, said a herd of more than 10 to 20 elephants, but about four, had entered the courtyard of Keo Seima district hall. He said that the entry of these elephants is always happening every year, because this place is its location before it has to come only this month.
Wild elephants in the Keo Seima District Wildlife Sanctuary are also confirmed to have appeared behind people's houses, along National Road 76, behind the military headquarters or the pond and pond in front of the Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary.
The director of the Mondulkiri Provincial Department of Environment also expressed concern that if they enter densely populated urban areas, when people chase or throw them, they may confront people. Mr. Keo Sopheak said, "Currently, the number of wild elephants in Mondulkiri is between 370 and 500, which is increasing, and the wild elephant in Mondulkiri is only one species, the Asian elephant."
