The Cambodia-Japan Friendship Bridge, also known as the first Chroy Changvar Bridge, has been repaired.
Speaking to reporters on March 26, the senior official of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport confirmed that the Cambodia-Japan Friendship Bridge has been completely repaired and is only waiting for the inauguration.
Recently, the Ministry of Public Works and Transport announced that the Cambodia-Japan Friendship Bridge, or Chroy Changvar I Bridge, is scheduled to reopen on April 3, 2019 under the presidency of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia. .
The Cambodia-Japan Friendship Bridge, 971 meters long and 13 meters wide, located along National Road 6A in Phnom Penh, was built and inaugurated in 1963 with a grant from Japan.
The bridge was damaged by the Cambodian Civil War and was rebuilt in 1992 by Japan and renamed the Cambodia-Japan Friendship Bridge by His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk.
Due to the age of the bridge, and as economic growth and traffic flow have steadily increased, the bridge needs to be repaired urgently at a cost of about 30 million US dollars under a grant from the Japanese government. The renovation process started on September 15, 2017. Cambodia Development Council approves investment project to open 3 new bags and garment factories with a total investment of over 23 million USD
