Japanese Ambassador: As of 2018, Japan has provided nearly 4 billion US dollars in assistance to Cambodia
Speaking on the occasion of the opening of the press conference on Japan ODA 2019 on March 26, 2019, the Ambassador of Japan stated: "In the last 30 years, we have been supporting Cambodia. From the first peace process through subsequent reconstruction to economic development. The Japanese government has provided about US $ 3.9 billion under ODA by fiscal 2018.
The Ambassador emphasized that Japan supports the strengthening of socio-economic foundations to help Cambodia achieve its status as a high middle-income country by 2030, focusing on three priority areas: 1: Industrial development, 2: Providing a better quality of life and 3: Promoting a sustainable society by strengthening governance.
He said Japan has been working with Cambodia in many areas. These include the construction of socio-economic infrastructure such as roads, ports, hospitals, schools, human resources and water supply systems, demining and unexploded ordnance, and investment promotion.
According to Katana, Japan's ODA Country Manager for Cambodia, Japan has been actively providing assistance to Cambodia since 1991. He said that the total amount of ODA to Cambodia as of 2017 (at the rate of 1 dollar / 110 yen) is about 1275 million US dollars in concessional loans, about 1831 million US dollars in grants and about 784 million US dollars. As technical cooperation.
At the same time, Mr. Horinouchi Hidehisa did not forget to extend a warm welcome to all journalists who participated in the three-day visit to the press conference on ODA Japan 2019 for three days. He said that through this important event, the Embassy of Japan and JICA are proud to have invited prominent Cambodian journalists to the area of Japan's ODA assistance program.
The Japanese Ambassador added that the invitation for journalists to visit 2019 is to help spread the word about the achievements of Cambodia-Japan cooperation. "It is very important that we make sure that our efforts are properly understood and supported by the people of Cambodia and Japan," he said. "Therefore, the role of the media is indispensable in achieving these goals."
