Mr. Sok Touch, President of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, said that for a year and a half, the Royal Academy of Cambodia has planted 25 hectares of seedlings, dug ponds to reserve wildlife in the dry season. The Royal Academy of Cambodia will set up a museum there because there are so many things in the park, and the Royal Academy of Cambodia will try to protect the wildlife there and make a center there to make the park more attractive.
Ms. Rasakrin said that the signing of the Memorandum of Understanding between companies from Sweden and the Royal Academy of Cambodia today is because the two countries have similar views on biodiversity protection, so it will be very important between the two countries. Both.
She added that Swedish private companies will be involved in developing more diverse natural resources in Techo Sen Russey Trap National Park. Obviously, in Swedish culture, forests are not only a gift from nature, but also an important point for the development of Sweden's national economy.
