As of Monday evening, nearly 660,000 Thais had signed the petition online, calling for the removal of all election commission members. In addition, the petition also aims to take legal action against the top five officials of the Thai Election Commission, including the chairman.
According to the organizers of the campaign, the petition will be submitted to the President of the Senate with the expectation that he will ask the Anti-Corruption Commission to lead an investigation into the five senior officials. But under Thai law, the upper house needs the support of at least 60 percent of its members to dissolve the election commission.
The Thai Election Commission (NEC) on Monday postponed the announcement of preliminary results, which were originally scheduled for 10:00 a.m. the same day, but were postponed to 2:00 p.m. Later at 4:00 pm on Monday, the commission announced that the full preliminary results will be announced this Friday.
Cambodian Red Cross Visits and Gives Gifts to Burned Child Victims and Condolences to Families of Siem Reap Car Robbers
(Siem Reap): On Monday, March 25, 2019, Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian Red Cross, assigned a working group to visit and Bringing a gift to a 10-year-old boy named Tem Moeut, who suffered burns all over his body due to an unscrupulous person who set fire to gasoline (allegedly stealing 10,000 Riels) on March 22, 2019.
The boy is currently living with his parents in Kouk Kanang village, Reul commune, Puok district, Siem Reap province, and is currently being treated at Kantha Bopha Angkor Hospital in Siem Reap province.
The families of the victims in the following two cases expressed their deepest excitement and gratitude to Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen for their efforts to alleviate the hardships and encourage the victims without discrimination. Nothing.
Please note that the materials provided for the families of injured children include: 30 kg of rice, 10 cans of fish, 1 box of noodles, 1 kit (mosquito net, blanket, scarf, sarong, T-shirt) and some money.
The Royal Government and the private sector jointly promote the use of the riel
(Phnom Penh): Currently, the use of the riel is increasing significantly due to the support of various sectors, both public and private.
Seeing the necessity and importance of the use of the riel in Cambodia, many large sectors in Cambodia, including: the Garment Manufacturers Association, the banking sector in Cambodia, the microfinance sector, etc. have joined together to promote the use of money. Riel.
He added that every year the association always negotiates to set a new minimum wage, and this new year will have some changes through the creation of a law for wages by setting up a National Wage Council.
"For the last two years, the Garment Manufacturers Association has been trying to propose a national currency wage (in riel), but it will take some time to attract more support from the Ministry of Economy and Finance," he said. Stuff. ”
"We support the setting of wages in riel," he said. "The riel is not only a national identity, but a policy tool that the Royal Government uses to boost our exports."
Dr. In Channy, President of the Association in Cambodia, said that in the past, the Association has always helped to promote and promote many uses, including paying taxes in riel, setting a credit limit of at least 10% in riel.
He added that in order to make the use of the riel more widespread, even with the participation of the public and the private sector, the riel has a stable circulation in the market.
Ms. Neang Chanthana, Deputy Governor of the National Bank of Cambodia, said that in order to promote the use of the riel, the National Bank of Cambodia will make efforts to promote based on market mechanisms and realities and be ready to cooperate with stakeholders to implement this work. Effective in accordance with the policy of the Royal Government.
It should be noted that the riel has maintained good stability in the last two decades and the demand for the riel has increased rapidly, with the currency increasing at an average rate of 16% per year and the riel deposit increasing by 26%. .
