On behalf of the Royal Government of Thailand, Lt. Gen. City Pitol Nim Nuon, the leadership of the Ministry of Defense and the Royal Thai Army, thanked Senior Minister Meas Sophea for providing valuable time to allow the Thai Mine Action Center (MRT) delegation. TMAC paid a courtesy call and discussed work in a sense to strengthen the cooperation between the Thai Mine Action Center (TMAC) and the Cambodian Mine Action Center (CMAC).
Mr. Sitthipol Nimnuon informed the Senior Minister that he had previously served as the Royal Thai Armed Forces in Cambodia, but has now been appointed by the Royal Thai Army Chief of Staff to the new position of Director General of the Mine Action Center. Thai TMAC.
The senior minister further stated that CMAC is only the operator responsible for clearing mines in the minefields that we know of, and that the border areas or areas not yet designated as minefields, we will continue to study the people or who knows. Areas with mines or unexploded ordnance to be cleared only if the land in Cambodia is depleted of landmines and unexploded ordnance.
The Senior Minister further stated that the problem of unexploded ordnance left over from the Cambodian Civil War is the greatest risk to the Cambodian people and all of Cambodia's victims, and that landmines and unexploded ordnance left over from the war in Cambodia Most of Cambodia in northwestern Cambodia borders Thailand.
He also thanked the Royal Government of Thailand for its past support for all activities of the Royal Government of Cambodia, especially the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, in training human resources in various fields.
This statement was made by Mr. Lun Chheng Kay at the opening ceremony of the training course on the examination of voter list and voter registration 2019 for officials representing political parties, non-governmental organizations, local authorities and PEC officials in Phnom Penh on March 24. 2019.
He said that according to the law, the NEC must update the voter list through the annual voter list and voter registration to ensure the quality of the voter list.
He said that in the process of reviewing the voter list and voter registration from year to year is not the same, there are always some changes depending on the principles, laws, regulations and procedures of the NEC.
According to the PEC official, updating the voter list is focused on:
1: Deletion of voters' names from the official voter list in 2018 for voters who have died or moved or their residences from communes / sangkats or are temporarily deprived of the right to vote based on clear documents and That's right.
2. Correction of voter data refers to the correction of name, gender, age, address or change of polling station in the commune / sangkat and other data at the request of the applicant based on the accuracy of the applicant's documents.
3: Computerized voter registration refers to the registration of citizens who have just reached the age of 18 as of July 31, 2019, those who were born before or until August 1, 2001 and Citizens who are 18 years of age or older are not yet on the official voter list in any commune / sangkat, as well as people who have moved to a commune / sangkat.
But according to some analysts, despite widespread expectations that Sunday's election will restore democracy as well as civilian rule, the Thai military will still play a key role in political decisions. Under the new constitution, incumbent Prime Minister Prayuth Chan-ocha remains hopeful to continue leading the elected government.
* The House of Representatives The composition of the 500-seat House of Representatives is what will be decided this Sunday, but not all seats will go through direct elections. That is, according to the new constitution, the House of Representatives has 350 seats, where voters can directly choose the candidate and party of their choice.
* Forms for party seats
The value of one seat in the House of Representatives is determined based on the total number of voters and the distribution of the 500 seats. For example, if 40 million people went to the polls on Sunday to get a seat in parliament, each party would have to get 80,000 votes.
* Election of the Prime Minister
A party must have at least 25 seats in the National Assembly in order to nominate a candidate for prime minister. Next, each candidate must win 370 of the 750 lawmakers and senators to become prime minister, as the Thai military regime will already have 250 seats in the Senate, while its ally, the Palang Pracharat party. The Thai military needs only 126 more seats in parliament.
In addition, in the event that a coalition or coalition partner is unable to agree on a new prime minister, the new constitution will allow for the election of a non-parliamentary prime minister.
(Rattanakiri): On the morning of March 24, 2019, Mr. Muong Pang, Deputy Governor of Rattanakiri Province, representing Mr. Thong Savun, Provincial Governor, presided over the 7th Clean City Day under the theme "Clean City, Warm City" at The premises of the Provincial Department of Culture and Fine Arts are located in Sangkat Boeung Kanseng, Banlung City, Ratanakkiri Province.
