China wants to help Cambodia build wind and solar power in addition to reliance on hydropower
The Chinese Deputy Foreign Minister made the remarks during a working meeting with Deputy Prime Minister Hor Namhong on the afternoon of March 21, 2019.
During the meeting, Deputy Prime Minister Hor Namhong asked the Chinese government to boost the purchase of 400,000 tons of rice from Cambodia, which Chinese President Xi Jinping informed Samdech Techo Hun Sen during his visit in January. In addition to bananas, Cambodian agricultural products such as mango, pepper and tamarind, which currently have farmers in Pailin and Battambang provinces are facing market problems.
As for the political situation, Hor Namhong told the Chinese Deputy Foreign Minister that there was no concern as the former opposition party abroad seemed to have lost its voice and that Cambodia had made economic and political reforms, including exemptions. Punish former CNRP members who can resume politics and form a new party.
Kong Xuanyou told Deputy Prime Minister Hor Namhong that the Chinese government has indicated its intention to expand further infrastructure development projects in Cambodia to be linked to the Belt and Road project.
The Deputy Minister also stated that the Chinese side is also able to help Cambodia to build clean electricity powered by wind and solar energy in addition to relying only on hydropower.
He said that in the case of Cambodian rice, the Chinese side has already studied, China can increase quotas for Cambodia every year, and in the future, China will promote direct imports for other agricultural products to reduce transportation costs.
In the case of the EBA, China's position is with Cambodia, with the Chinese side studying ways to help Cambodia ease pressure from the European Union. China strongly opposes the European Union, which has always used the EBA as a political tool, and the Chinese side understands that Europe does not fully agree on the Cambodian case. China will push for the start of "free trade agreement" negotiations with Cambodia.
