Reminiscent of the major political events of the last two decades in Thailand ahead of this weekend's general election
BANGKOK: Over the past two decades, Thailand has experienced two coups, countless demonstrations and political instability. But the economy remains strong as Southeast Asia's second-largest economy prepares for general elections on Sunday, March 24, for the first time since the 2014 coup.
The following are the dates of major events in Thailand:
* 2010: Red Shirts protest again in Bangkok for months until a military crackdown that kills 90 people and this is a violent clash between authorities and protesters that left many dead. Most since 1992.
2013: Anti-government protesters, led by a top Democrat leader, take to the streets in Bangkok after the Yingluck government drafted an amnesty law that was seen as intended to open The way for her brother to return to Thailand.
2018 - The Thai military regime lifts the political ban it imposed after coming to power.
