(Preah Sihanouk): The traffic police authority of Preah Sihanouk Provincial Police has once again reminded drivers of all types of vehicles to increase their compliance with the road traffic law on a regular basis without speeding. Do not overtake each other, lack caution.
Deputy Police Commissioner for Land Traffic Planning of Sihanoukville Provincial Police, Mr. I Sokha, said that traffic accidents caused by all kinds of vehicles on National Road 4 and downtown Sihanoukville continue to occur almost every day. Injury and death.
He added that the rate of traffic accidents that occurred in Sihanoukville, especially along National Road 4, is still a worrying sign because this accident has occurred continuously. And that in this case, his traffic police force also continued to check the implementation of the road traffic law on a daily basis.
Mr. E Sokha acknowledged that some road users or drivers did not regularly obey the traffic law by driving at excessive speeds. Overtaking in dangerous roads and driving under the influence of alcohol. And that every day, most traffic accidents are caused by human factors rather than vehicle factors.
However, Mr. I Sokha did not confirm the number of traffic accidents in the first quarter of 2019 as of March 22, the number of traffic accidents occurred, but only briefly stated that traffic accidents in the province. Sihanoukville continued to occur almost daily.
Starting from this issue, Mr. I Sokha this morning led his colleagues to the closed special economic zone to continue education and dissemination on road traffic law and participate in the program to distribute driver's licenses and provide guidance to driving a total of 150 workers' trucks. Increase awareness of the effects of traffic accidents.
On that occasion, Mr. I Sokha and his colleagues also distributed 67 driver's licenses to drivers of trucks transporting workers to and from the Sihanoukville Special Economic Zone (category B 33 and category D2 34).
Regarding the accident, Mr. I Sokha said that on March 20, a truck carrying bricks down the slope of Yeay Mao in front of the train station near the port of Sihanoukville also collided with a Lexus car. Two vehicles then crashed into the houses of people living nearby, causing property damage and injuring several people.
On March 21, there was another traffic accident on National Road 4 in Prey Nob district, causing damage to cars and injuries to some people.
