This statement was made by Minister Mam Bun Heng during the opening ceremony of the Diabetes Project for Children and Adults at the Cambodiana Hotel on March 21, 2019.
Minister Mam Bunheng continued, "Non-communicable diseases are the silent, vicious disease that is the killer in disguise, killing people, now more and more among the general population." "It is a public health issue that requires all of us, all relevant institutions, to pay attention and make non-communicable diseases a priority."
In addition, Minister Mam Bunheng said that most of the non-communicable diseases can not be cured, but can be prevented up to 80% if we change the habits or behaviors that face four major risks. Quit smoking, quit or reduce harmful alcohol consumption, eat a healthy diet and exercise regularly and regularly.
Minister Mam Bunheng stated that "In response to the increasing number of non-communicable diseases, especially focusing on diabetes and high blood pressure, the Ministry of Health has provided additional training to health officials working in hospitals. "In 17 provinces, we currently have diabetes services in 28 diabetes clinics and are expanding."
At the same time, the Ministry has also trained officials in health centers on counseling, blood tests, blood sugar testing, how to send referrals to 64 communities. This training and equitable service is in line with the recommendation of the Prime Minister of Cambodia Samdech Techo Hun Sen, who spoke at the closing ceremony of the 40th meeting of the Ministry of Health recently.
At the same time, the Minister of Health also called on health officials at all levels to work together to promote and support the diabetic services to work well for the people with high efficiency. In particular, to continue to educate people to understand how to live. Regularly to get healthy.
