Deputy Prime Minister Ke Kim Yan wonders that in some cases only drugs were seized and people were never caught
(Phnom Penh): Deputy Prime Minister Ke Kim Yan, President of the National Authority for Combating Drugs, said with suspicion that in some drug busts, the authorities only seized drugs, while people never caught them.
The head of the National Authority for Combating Drugs stated: "Some cases only seize drugs, but people do not know where? In some cases, 10 kilograms of drugs do not know who owns them. Thus, in the case of research, it is a crime to produce traffic, store, distribute and use.
Deputy Prime Minister Ke Kim Yan made this statement on the morning of March 20, 2019, when he opened the conference on the results of drug control work in 2018 and the direction of implementation of the National Authority for Combating Drugs in 2019. Office of the Ministry of Interior.
The head of the National Anti-Drug Authority said that drug-related crimes occur in five ways: production, circulation, storage, distribution and consumption. He urged the relevant authorities to use these five crime methods to find out who the mastermind of the crime was.
Deputy Prime Minister Ke Kim Yan added, "So, in the case of 10 kilograms of drugs, where are they produced locally or imported from foreign countries? Where to find it .... "When there is a drug case, you have to use those five points to research, otherwise it will disappear."
Deputy Prime Minister Ke Kim Yan also expressed his deep appreciation to the authorities for successfully launching a major crackdown. He warned that those involved in drug cases should be prepared, but did not say what action would be taken.
