The Minister of Agriculture issued seven recommendations to promote the effective and efficient implementation of the law on the management of pesticides and fertilizers.
(Siem Reap): Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, on the afternoon of March 18, 2019, attended the closing ceremony of the meeting to review the results of agricultural legislation in 2018 and work direction for 2019-2020.
On this occasion, in order to achieve positive results in fulfilling the unit's duties on the management of agricultural materials, especially to promote the effective and efficient implementation of the law on the management of pesticides and fertilizers, the Minister gave some recommendations to the organization. The whole ceremony, as well as all stakeholders, pay attention to the following:
First, continue to prepare legal documents under the Law on the Management of Pesticides and Fertilizers. Coordinate with specialized units to prepare, review, revise and update various legal documents by adapting to the situation in the context of the country's development.
Second, to study the possibility of reducing costs by improving cost-effective services that can increase competition in both domestic and foreign markets and continue to improve the quality and safety of agricultural inputs. Simplify the inspection procedures and registration procedures for pesticides and fertilizers to be more effective. Strengthen and expand the capacity of the laboratory, both human and technical resources to examine, analyze and evaluate the quality of agricultural inputs.
Third, we must continue to promote inspections and strengthen law enforcement on commercial activities, especially to prevent the influx of illegal pesticides and fertilizers. Check the quality standards of information labels in Khmer on the packaging and legal origin.
Sixth, to promote good governance in providing transparent public services to agricultural equipment traders by strengthening the will to work and adhere to dignified discipline in the performance of their roles and responsibilities based on the relevant working principles and procedures in force. . Must strive to learn regularly about the relevant laws, procedures and technical rules in the management of agricultural equipment to be a competent unit.
Seventh, both management and professional officers must strengthen the capacity, quality of work and professional conscience to improve efficiency and effectiveness. The popularity and delicious taste of Cambodian rice makes rice exports do not fall despite tax from Europe (Video inside)
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Nong Veasna, Vice President of the Cambodian Rice Federation, confirmed that although Cambodian rice is subject to European tariffs, Cambodia's rice exports are still on the rise due to the popularity and deliciousness of rice. Cambodia.
Mr. Nong Veasna made this statement in an interview with the program "Special Guest of the Week" on "The situation of Cambodia's rice exports after the European Union removed the preferential tariff system."
"Cambodia's rice exports in the first two months of 2019 increased slightly compared to 2018, despite the impact of the removal of preferential tariffs on Cambodian rice from Europe," he said. By. "We should look at the overall situation, whether we have to rely on any market or we should strengthen our production capacity even more."
He added that Europe has levied a tax on rice from Cambodia of 175 euros (about $ 200) and has been valid since January 16, 2019, when we were taxed for two months, but instead exported. Our overall increase was small, not as slow as some people think, but on the contrary, Cambodian rice is still growing.
He added, "Of course, Cambodian rice exports to Europe have declined, but the popularity of Cambodian rice and the delicious taste of Cambodian rice exports have not declined, despite a narrow market, Cambodian rice remains. "It can be overcome because of other growth as well as other markets that recognize the taste and quality of Cambodian rice, such as the quality of rice produced by farmers."
The vice president of the federation said that although rice exports from Cambodia to Europe fell by more than 10,000 tons, the decline was offset by the Chinese market and growth in other markets. In that, China alone imported more than one Cambodian rice from the European Union, which has nearly 30 EU countries.
Notably, in the first two months, Cambodia exported 33,969 tonnes of rice to the European Union, while the European Union began to impose tariffs on Cambodian rice for three years from January 16, 2019. While China provided a quota of 400,000 tonnes of rice to Cambodia, in the first two months, China imported 43,452 tonnes of Cambodian rice.
