Official letter of Mr. Riel Khemrin: Request for political rehabilitation and the formation of a new political party, the Royal Decree granting him the right to do politics will be issued today (with letter)
(Phnom Penh): According to the official letter of Mr. Riel Khemrin, former Member of Parliament of the former National Rescue Party, which was received by Fresh News this morning, March 17, 2019, it shows that Mr. Riel Khemrin has submitted two official letters. To Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Interior, to request political rehabilitation, with one handwriting and the other signed.
In his official letter, Riel Khemrin stated that he intends to form a new political party to contest the upcoming 2022-2023 election. Mr. Riel Kheamrin submitted a letter to the Ministry of Interior on March 14, 2019.
Riel Khemrin, a former CNRP lawmaker, was one of 118 former CNRP leaders banned by the Supreme Court for five years in November 2017.
It should be noted that Mr. Riel Khemrin is the fourth former leader of the Cambodia National Rescue Party to apply for political rights after Mr. Sim Sovanny alias Sim Vanny, a former member of the Board of Directors of the former National Rescue Party.
Sim Sovanny is the third politician among former CNRP leaders to receive political rehabilitation, after Kong Koam, a former senior CNRP adviser, and Kong Bora, a former CNRP lawmaker. King on January 15, 2019. Mr. Sim Sovanny is ranked 44th on the Supreme Court's list of political bans.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol leads nearly 700 people in Koh Thom district on a train trip from Phnom Penh to Kampong Chhnang province (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, leads this morning, March 17, 2019 Nearly 700 people from Koh Thom district, Kandal province, take the train from Phnom Penh to Kampong Chhnang.
Arranging for the people of Koh Thom to travel by train for the fourth time to promote support for travel by train north of Phnom Penh (Phnom Penh-Poipet), which the Royal Government of Cambodia fully reconnected on July 4, 2018, after failing to operate for many years.
Bringing people on the train also wants to show people the government's efforts to restore Cambodia's rail system, which was disrupted and destroyed during the war. In addition, I want the people of Koh Thom to know the taste of traveling by train, most of them have never traveled by train.
It should be noted that the railway section north of Phnom Penh from Phnom Penh to Poipet (Cambodia-Thailand border) with a total length of 386 km was built between 1929 to 1942 during the French colonial period. But the railway was discontinued during the Cambodian war of the 1970s.
The section of railway from Poipet to Phnom Penh was completely rehabilitated and reopened on July 4, 2018. The rehabilitation of the railway from Phnom Penh to Poipet, Banteay Meanchey province has cost about 96.6 million US dollars. Of that, about $ 13 million belongs to the Asian Development Bank and another $ 83.6 million is from the Royal Government.
