APSARA Authority restores Stung Preah Srok to maintain water growth and avoid floods
(Siem Reap): Stung Preah Srok is a natural river that has existed since ancient times and is located in Lvea commune, Puok district, Siem Reap province, with a total length of 5,800 meters. This river plays an important role like the Rolous River in Siem Reap because it liberates the flood from Angkor and Siem Reap city. In addition, it serves as a reservoir for people to use and farm in the community of Lvea commune.
Mr. Ram Vichet, Technical Officer of Water Management Department of APSARA Authority, said that APSARA Authority had already restored the upper Preah Srok River in 2015 with a length of 2 km. In addition to rehabilitating the river, the APSARA Authority has also built dams on both sides of the river as roads for travel, built a bridge and built a sluice gate to close or open traffic lights and release water for use according to the needs of the people.
Mr. Vichet said that the second rehabilitation of 1,500 meters will take three months, from February 12 to May 2019 to supply enough water for the expansion of agricultural land of more than 300 people. Hita. APSARA Authority has expanded the size and depth from the bottom of the old river from 1 meter to 1.50 meters according to the soil conditions of the landfill, including the width of the bottom 17 meters equal to the length of the upper river.
Once restored, the drainage system will be able to store more water, facilitate waterway traffic by using small boats during the rainy season, and the water flows to the Tonle Sap faster. He also requested the people to take good care of this river, it is strictly forbidden to pour garbage or sewage into the river, which leads to dirty water and shallow river.
It should be reminded that in 2012, APSARA Authority restored the drainage system to avoid flooding in Angkor area as well as Siem Reap city as a whole, which diverted a large part of the water to Puok district, including the restoration of various canal systems down to Stung Preah Srok in 2015 has a total length of 38 kilometers to release water from Siem Reap and store water for farming.
