Experts want to see the press adhere to high ethics in promoting drug users
(Phnom Penh): The Club of Cambodian Journalists and KHANA organized a meeting on the morning of March 14, 2019 to disseminate the Code of Conduct for Journalists to report on drug-affected people in a participatory manner. From many journalists from some institutions in the country.
Mr. Chuob Chamroeun, President of KHANA, raised legal issues related to drug use. He said that drug users who want to seek medical treatment can tell the authorities about their intentions, and if the authorities detain the person, they are violating the law.
At the same time, Mr. Chuob Chamroeun encouraged journalists to report on what makes people think that drug users are victims, not criminals. He said that if journalists could find a healer, give interviews to produce more information, it would be better because that information would become a model for other drug users to be more confident in seeking treatment.
Mr. Chhay Sophal, a journalism professor, urged journalists to take pictures related to drug users in a blurred manner before publishing them to hide their identities. He cited the consequences of saying that if a journalist published an information openly to a drug user, it would have an impact on him.
Mr. Chhay Sophal gave the example that if a man recovers from drugs and goes to work in the society, suddenly seeing pictures of his past on the bad press will make him feel depressed and ashamed of others. Let him go back to using drugs. Therefore, it is important to think in terms of drugs.
The draft resolution was approved by the US Senate by a vote of 54 to 46, with the aim of ending all US involvement in the Saudi-led war in Yemen.
That support includes targeting assistance for Saudi air operations and the provision of air refueling services to Saudi warplanes. The draft resolution will now be passed by the US House of Representatives.
The United States, a longtime ally of Saudi Arabia, began quietly providing support to the Saudi-led coalition in 2016 by providing intelligence, arms sales and refueling. Support has fluctuated since the outbreak of the murder of journalist Jamal Kashoggi.
A lucky Khmer Muslim man in Ratanakkiri province won 100 million Riel from Egyptian green tea
(Rattanak Kiri): The prize money of 100 million Riel from Echitan Green Tea Season 3 continued to find another lucky winner of its grand prize named Ker Kamary, male, 30 years old, working as a farm laborer, currently living Ten village, Ten commune, Kon Mom district, Rattanakiri province.
He told the team, which is a representative of the company handed over the prize money to the resident at the above address, that last weekend, after returning from the farm and feeling thirsty, he bought Echinacea green tea with Kulen fruit flavor from vendors near the market to drink.
When you turn the bottle cap, you suddenly see a prize of 100 million Riel in the bottle cap. At first sight, he was very excited and told his brother and wife and called the company via the phone number on the bottle cap.
Mr. Kamary said that the family life today is very difficult because they can not earn anything other than working as a farm laborer, but now Echitan has completely changed his family life for the better.
He added that after receiving a grand prize of 100 million riel from Echitan green tea, he will use it to treat his mother and save some money to build a house and buy land for a cashew plantation to support his future life.
He also thanked Echitan Green Tea Company for creating a great program that could change his family's destiny for the better.
