(Phnom Penh): On the morning of March 14, 2019, the Korea International Cooperation Agency (KOICA) signed an agreement with the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports to officially launch the project "Creating a new business training system between schools and industry in Cambodia.
The project is designed to build start-up ecosystems and marketing networks in Cambodia by creating a system to drive the creation of new companies at Cambodia's leading universities.
KOIKA will build the Cambodia-Korea New Business Training Center (CKIC) and support capacity building for the New Business Start-up Center, and promote support for the Start-up Program at the Royal University of Phnom Penh (RUPP) and the Institute of Technology of Cambodia (ITC). ) With a total budget of approximately US $ 7 million for a period of 5 years from 2019 to 2023.
At the signing ceremony, Ms. Bek Suk Hee said that Cambodia has maintained its economic growth at an average of 7% and is pursuing a policy to diversify its workforce structure. She stated that this project will contribute to bridging the gap between academic capacity and market demand to promote sustainable industrialization in Cambodia.
In addition, the main importance of this project, it will create a lot of high quality work will encourage more professional professionals in the industry, while the agency plans to lay the foundation for industrial expansion in developing countries. To strengthen national competitiveness from a long-term perspective.
