During the visit, Moon Jae-in will meet with the King of Cambodia at the Royal Palace and will also meet with Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia.
President Moon Jae-in and Samdech Techo Hun Sen will discuss boosting bilateral relations and expanding cooperation in various fields. The two sides also exchanged views on regional and international issues of common concern and interest. After the meeting, the two leaders will witness the signing ceremony of a number of agreements and make joint press releases.
President Moon Jae-in and Prime Minister Hun Sen will also address the Cambodia-Republic of Korea Business Forum to encourage more investment, economic and trade exchanges between the two countries.
President Moon Jae-in's first visit to Cambodia is another milestone in Cambodia-ROK relations, which will strengthen friendship and deepen cooperation between the two countries.
However, after the decision under a clear vision, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, a former outstanding diplomat of Cambodia, relations between Cambodia and South Korea have improved from year to year.
To enhance relations between the two countries, Samdech Hun Sen paid six official visits to South Korea, including in 1996, 2001, 2006, 2008, 2009 and 2014. In particular, South Korean leaders have also made frequent visits to Cambodia. Two South Korean presidents have visited Cambodia.
Starting from the good diplomatic relations, the trade relations between Cambodia and South Korea are also good. Trade volume between the two countries has steadily increased.
In particular, Cambodia's most exported goods to the Republic of Korea include: clothing, electronics, footwear, beverages, rubber bags, medicines and cardboard. In 2017, Cambodia also exported a total of 1,648 tons of agricultural products, including corn, mangoes, mushrooms, rubber, nuts, beans, pineapples, bananas, bananas, flour, coconut milk, honey, processed wood, tea leaves. French mats, cashew nuts, pepper and palm sugar.
South Korea is also a major investor in Cambodia. From 1996 to January 2019, 214 projects were invested by South Korean investors with a total value of about $ 4.47 billion. Of these, 123 are factories with a total investment of 486 million US dollars, creating about 80,000 jobs.
The Republic of Korea is also a major contributor to Cambodia's development, focusing on four priority areas: transportation, water resources management and public health, education and rural development. South Korea is the fourth largest donor of development aid to Cambodia after China, Japan and the Asian Development Bank (ADB).
In addition to grants, South Korea has also provided a number of development loans for a number of major projects. The report shows that between 2001 and 2018, the Republic of Korea provided $ 763.18 million in loans to implement 21 development projects, of which 10 are in progress and 11 have been completed.
For 2016-2018, the Republic of Korea has decided to provide a $ 350 million loan to implement a number of major projects. Three projects worth $ 160.58 million include: Irrigation and Flood Reduction Development Project in Banteay Meanchey, National Road 21 Takhmao-Chrey Thom Improvement Phase 2, and Integrated Wastewater System Development in Takhmao City.
Cambodia is also a destination for South Korean tourists. In 2017, Cambodia received about 345,081 South Korean tourists, and in 2018 there were 301,770. Korean tourists ranked fifth after China, Vietnam, Laos and Thailand. Currently, there are 5 airlines operating direct flights to Cambodia and South Korea, a total of 50 flights per week. The five direct airlines are: 4 Korean and 1 Cambodian.
In 2018, there were a total of 31,701 Cambodian workers in the Republic of Korea, 6,833 females, of which 4,870 new workers in 2018, 1,142 females. Workers receive an average monthly salary of about $ 1,430. A group of 10 workers who tried to encroach on the state forest land illegally and took it as their own were stopped by the officials of the Ministry of Environment
In addition, the Minister of Health requested that the organization help promote and review the inclusion of health education measures to combat the growing non-communicable diseases in public health. In particular, help train Cambodian health officials.
He added that the organization's project is also considering providing initiative initiatives to support malaria eradication volunteers in line with Cambodia's strategic policy to promote the implementation of the plan. More specifically, the problem of drug resistance.
