Japan provides over 260,000 USD for canal system rehabilitation and study environment in Oddar Meanchey and Battambang provinces
(Phnom Penh): The Government of Japan has agreed to provide a total of US $ 262,750 to three entities under the Non-Construction Assistance Scheme for Small-Scale Human Security Project (Kusanoni Assistance) for the rehabilitation of the canal system and the improvement of the learning environment in Cambodia. Oddar Meanchey and Battambang provinces.
The grant agreement was signed on March 13, 2019 with the participation of Mr. Horino Uji Hidehisa, Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary of Japan to Cambodia and representatives of the three recipients.
According to Horinouchi Hidehisa, the first project for the Oddar Meanchey Provincial Department of Water Resources and Meteorology, the Japanese government will provide about $ 89,000 for the rehabilitation of the O'Snok Dam.
He said about 1,300 people were expected to receive stable irrigation water and the amount of irrigated farmland was also expanded under the restored irrigation system.
Mr. Horinouchi Hidehisa said that the second project for the Department of Water Resources and Meteorology of Battambang Province, the Japanese government provided about 89,000 US dollars for the restoration of Boeung Khong irrigation system. The project is expected to benefit about 230 people in the area.
"I hope that the restored irrigation system will contribute to providing more irrigation water to rice fields and increasing agricultural production," added the Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary of Japan to Cambodia.
Separately, the latest project for the Techo Sen Regional Polytechnic Institute in Battambang, the Government of Japan provided about 85,000 US dollars to expand school facilities and install training equipment in the Techo Sen Regional Polytechnic Institute Battambang.
"Through this project, the capacity of 300 teachers and students will be strengthened and will help update their technical skills required by the relevant industries and the labor market." This is an additional emphasis of Horinouchi Hidehisa.
It should be noted that Japan's Kusanoni non-construction assistance was started in Cambodia in 1991 to support Cambodia's efforts to develop and rebuild the country at the grassroots level. Kampong Cham Provincial Commissioner Orders Police Officers to Take Care of Professional Skills, Strengthen and Monitor the Remaining Work
