The introductory speech of Samdech Techo Prime Minister was made on the occasion of the closing ceremony of the meeting to summarize the work results in 2018 and raise the direction for 2019 of the Ministry of Health on the afternoon of March 12, 2019.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that in order for the Cambodian health system to become popular and trusted by the people through quality, effective and virtuous treatment, reduce the use of health services abroad. To lose a lot of time and money, Samdech asked the Ministry of Health, relevant ministries and local authorities at all levels to work together to implement the following eight important tasks:
Second, to jointly prepare and effectively implement human resource development strategies in the health sector to strengthen professional capacity through pre-service and post-service training, especially to jointly develop a health human resource management database. Deployment of qualified health workers strengthens incentives for health workers, especially in remote rural areas.
Third, to jointly implement measures to prevent and prevent diseases by increasing cooperation with communities and stakeholders to educate and promote hygiene, food safety, health care and the effects of alcohol, drugs, tobacco and its effects. Affected by climate change.
Fourth, to jointly prepare and strengthen the implementation of laws and regulations related to the control of the use of drugs without a prescription, the use of chemicals in food and the use of alcohol and tobacco.
Fifth, to jointly establish multi-sectoral mechanisms for nutrition promotion interventions with the participation of local communities and sub-national administrations in high-risk areas for malnutrition.
Sixth, to jointly promote the development of social health protection wives by increasing the coverage of the Health Equity Fund, the social security system, health care for public officials, workers of the economic sector in the system and the legal framework and mechanism for launching the insurance system. Social health for people in the informal economy.
