BEIJING: Over the past 40 years, people in Beijing, the capital of China, have planted a total of more than 200 million trees after the National Tree Planting Day was established, according to a report. The first time was on March 12, 1979. This was released by the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Tuesday evening, March 12, 2019.
According to the latest figures, in 2018, Beijing covers an area of 16,808 square kilometers and has a total population of 21.54 million. For years, some international environmental organizations have considered Beijing one of the most toxic cities in the world due to its booming industrial activity and population growth.
But recent reports, especially images from NASA satellites, have taken the international community by surprise as China's green cover has risen sharply compared to 20 years ago. Demonstrates the success of China's forest cover conservation and enhancement, which has been helping to restore environmental sustainability in the face of smog and global warming crises.
