(Banteay Meanchey): The light criminal police on March 11, 2019, exposed a suspect who forged public documents (forged passports) for migrant workers to find work in Thailand.
Colonel Khem Sophorn, director of the Minor Criminal Police Office, said that the suspect, Im Samphors, 38, resided in Kilometer 4 village, Phsar Kandal commune, Poipet city and was a laborer.
The director of the office confirmed that after complaints from many victims who were workers who applied for passports to work in Thailand, when the Thai authorities checked the documents as fake and were arrested by the Thai side. That back.
Banteay Meanchey Provincial Police Commissioner Major General Ace Cam issued an order to the Poipet City Police Inspectorate and the Poipet City Inspectorate to launch an investigation and arrest the suspect, Im Samphors, at 3:50 p.m. In the afternoon of March 7, 2019 in Poipet city.
Colonel Khem Sophorn said that after the arrest and interrogation, the suspect admitted that he was the one who stamped the extension of the expired workers' passports, which had been done since 2017.
From 2011 to 2013, he worked at the Cambodian Embassy in Bangkok, delaying his passport when he returned to Poipet, buying a set of embassy stamps. Based on the victim's claim as a laborer and the confession.
The suspects, along with exhibits, were sent to the provincial court by the specialized force to prepare the case for legal proceedings. Preah Vihear Provincial Governor: In 2019, Preah Vihear Province will produce organic rice for sale to companies according to the contract and there will be an increase
(Preah Vihear): Mr. Un Chanda, Governor of Preah Vihear Province, expressed his expectation that in 2019, the production of organic rice by the community to sell to companies under contract will increase. At the same time, Mr. Un Chanda also requested all communities to produce organic rice with sincerity.
Mr. Un Chanda made this remark during the signing ceremony of the Memorandum of Understanding and the contract for the production and supply of organic rice between Preah Vihear Meanchey Agricultural Community Union and Golden Rice (Cambodia) Co., Ltd. at the Provincial Department of Agriculture this morning. This year 2019.
On that occasion, Mr. Un Chanda said that the achievement of the organic rice production contract that the Union of Agricultural Communities and Agricultural Communities in Preah Vihear province has supplied to export companies has achieved good results.
He said that in 2018, 34 farming communities with about 5,000 producer families signed contracts to produce and supply organic rice to exporters in the amount of nearly 12,000 tons.
In particular, in 2019, with the increase in the number of farmers produced by Golden Rice (Cambodia) Co., Ltd., which will sign a contract to buy organic rice from the Preah Vihear Meanchey Agricultural Community Union, Mr. Un Chanda expressed the expectation that both agricultural communities The supply of organic rice increased from 15,000 tons to 20,000 tons.
On behalf of the Preah Vihear Provincial Administration, Mr. Un Chanda expressed his deep gratitude to Golden Rice (Cambodia) Co., Ltd. as well as all development partners who have always supported the development of the agricultural sector in Preah Vihear under all circumstances.
He hoped that this cooperation will continue the good tradition to contribute to reducing poverty to the maximum in line with the direction of the Millennium Development Goals in Cambodia, as well as the Royal Government's poverty reduction policy.
Mr. Peng Trida, Director of Preah Vihear Provincial Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, said that there are currently 43 agricultural communities in Preah Vihear Province, coordinated by the Agricultural Community Development Office and registered as legal agricultural communities by the Department of Agriculture and Forestry. Hunting and fishing in Preah Vihear province.
The director of the Preah Vihear Provincial Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries confirmed that in 2018, the agricultural community across the province has a total of 9,285 members.
