(Kampong Speu): Mr. Thong Khon, Minister of Tourism, said that with the increase of tourists who like to go on adventures in the mountains, the Ministry of Tourism has been preparing natural waterfalls to be a standard adventure tourism area. Proper community in addition to the existing potential of the resort.
The Minister confirmed this during a meeting with the community of eco-tourism waterfall in Taing Samrong village, Omlaing commune, Thpong district, Kampong Speu province on March 10, 2019. This community was established in 2001.
This announcement means that in addition to other ecotourism sites such as the Jiphat ecotourism community and the Areng community in Koh Kong province, the Kampong Phluk community in Siem Reap, Cambodia will have another waterfall ecotourism community in Kampong Speu to serve the needs of The number of tourists is increasing every year.
He advised the standardization team and the tour guide training team that recruiting people to become tour guides must select the people in the community because the people there already own the district. Know the roads and geography in their area better and they will also earn extra income from this work.
In addition to setting community standards in the waterfall natural resort area, Minister Thong Khon also asked all parties, especially the Association of Travel Agents of Cambodia to help promote tourism in the area to tourists more widely.
In addition, he also asked the relevant ministries and departments to help improve and build more infrastructure, such as telephone-Internet service, roads connecting National Road 53, electricity, clean water for people and tourists to use.
The active minister also urged the Chreav community, if there is any deforestation, wildlife hunting, to stop immediately to preserve natural resources for tourism, because tourists always want to see what is in Those communities and protected areas, without all the natural resources, no tourists come to visit and the community itself has no money to deal with daily life.
